course: dictionary
lesson: 11514
title: Nature words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name plants you may never have seen. Context can help. «By the river, the alders dipped their branches into the water.» You can picture a tree near water, so an alder must be a tree that likes wet ground.
explain: «Mia found small brown cones on the alder all winter.» The cones are the clue. Alders make little woody cones that stay on the tree through the cold months. Ask: what word would fit here? A tree is a good guess, and the dictionary confirms it.
explain: Science notes add more clues. Alder roots help hold river banks together, so the word often appears near water, wet soil or wood. When you meet alder, look at the words around it for water, rivers or wet ground.
fact: An alder is a tree of the birch family.
fact: Alders grow by rivers and in wet ground.
fact: Alders have roundish leaves, small cones and catkins.
fact: Alder wood does not rot easily under water, so it was used for posts and piers.
fact: Venice stands partly on alder, so alder wood helps hold up buildings in water.
exercise: «By the river, the alders dipped their branches into the water.» What is an alder? | *A tree that grows by rivers and in wet ground | A small fish that swims in rivers | A flower that grows on hills | A boat used for fishing
exercise: «Mia found small brown cones on the alder all winter.» What does winter tell you about the cones? | They fall off in the spring | They turn bright red in the cold | *They stay on the tree all winter | Birds eat them every day
exercise: «Alder wood does not rot easily under water.» What does this tell you about alder? | It turns green in the sun | *It lasts well in water | It is very soft and light | It floats away quickly
test: «Alders grow in wet ground, so you will often find them near a river.» What does alder mean? | A kind of fish | A stone used for building | A bird with long legs | *A tree that grows by rivers and in wet ground
test: Which word in this sentence names a tree? «The alder leaned over the water and dropped its cones.» | leaned | *alder | cones | water
exam: Lena reads, «Alder roots hold the river bank together.» Why are alders good trees to grow near rivers? | *Their roots help hold the banks together | They only grow in dry sand | Their cones make the river flow faster | Their leaves are soft enough to drink
